7475: @code{mapfile} builtin when no variable name is supplied.
7476: 
7477: @item OLDPWD
7478: The previous working directory as set by the @code{cd} builtin.
7479: 
7480: @item OPTERR
7481: If set to the value 1, Bash displays error messages
7482: generated by the @code{getopts} builtin command.
7483: @env{OPTERR}
7484: is initialized to 1 each time the shell is invoked.
7485: 
7486: @item OSTYPE
7487: A string describing the operating system Bash is running on.
7488: 
7489: @item PIPESTATUS
7490: An array variable (@pxref{Arrays})
7491: containing a list of exit status values from the commands
7492: in the most-recently-executed foreground pipeline, which may
7493: consist of only a simple command
7494: (@pxref{Shell Commands}).
7495: Bash sets
7496: @code{PIPESTATUS}
7497: after executing multi-element pipelines,
7498: timed and negated pipelines,
7499: simple commands,
7500: subshells created with the @samp{(} operator,
7501: the
7502: @code{[[}
7503: and
7504: @code{((}
7505: compound commands, and
7506: after error conditions that result in the
7507: shell aborting command execution.
7508: 
7509: @item POSIXLY_CORRECT
7510: If this variable is in the environment when Bash starts, the shell
7511: enters @sc{posix} mode (@pxref{Bash POSIX Mode}) before reading the
7512: startup files, as if the @option{--posix} invocation option had been supplied.
7513: If it is set while the shell is running, Bash enables @sc{posix} mode,
7514: as if the command
7515: @example
7516: @code{set -o posix}
7517: @end example
7518: @noindent
7519: had been executed.
7520: When the shell enters @sc{posix} mode, it sets this variable if it was
7521: not already set.
7522: 
7523: @item PPID
7524: The process @sc{id} of the shell's parent process.
7525: This variable is readonly.
7526: 
7527: @item PROMPT_COMMAND
7528: If this variable is set, and is an array,
7529: the value of each set element is interpreted as a command to execute
7530: before printing the primary prompt (@env{$PS1}).
7531: If this is set but not an array variable,
